ssure effects. Together, these mechanism nodes deepen the community from “what symptoms occur” to “why symptoms may occur” in specific intracranial tumor contexts. [Data: Entities (5522, 232); Relationships (12331)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Headaches are represented as a cross-context symptom linked to broader symptom categorization\",\n      \"explanation\": \"HEADACHES are defined as a symptom that can arise in several medical contexts, including as a common symptom in glioma/brain tumor settings and in leukemia when disease involves or spreads to the fluid around the brain and spinal cord (cerebrospinal fluid). The relationship data explicitly links HEADACHES into the SYMPTOMS hub as one of the symptoms that may occur with leukemia involving surrounding fluid. [Data: Entities (1888, 232); Relationships (3199)]\\n\\nThis placement highlights headaches as a non-specific symptom that gains interpretive value depending on clinical context—consistent with the community’s overall symptom-centric orientation, where symptom presence contributes to suspicion and further evaluation rather than serving as a standalone diagnosis. [Data: Entities (1888, 232); Relationships (3199)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Jaundice is singled out as a symptom that can raise suspicion for pancreatic cancer while remaining non-specific\",\n      \"explanation\": \"JAUNDICE is defined as yellowed skin or eyes and is explicitly described as a symptom that can prompt suspicion of pancreatic cancer while also potentially being caused by other health conditions. A relationship directly connects JAUNDICE to the SYMPTOMS hub, reinforcing it as an example symptom used in clinical suspicion pathways. [Data: Entities (3118, 232); Relationships (6168)]\\n\\nThis illustrates a broader pattern in the community: certain symptoms act as clinical triggers for targeted concern (here, pancreatic cancer suspicion), while retaining differential diagnosis ambiguity—aligning with the repor